Update:

Since everyone was so helpful with their suggestions, I thought I'd
give a bit of an update on my status.  I descended to the Mine's End
as suggested, and with my small army of kitties I had few problems. 
After a bit, I located a few polytraps, and with some effort I managed
to make my pets more useful - I got a Bone Devil and a Horned Devil,
if I recall correctly.  Unfortunately, the Bone Devil immediately
stepped on the trap again and became a gelatinous cube, and again
several more times becoming progressively less useful each time.  By
the time I got it free, it was a baby long worm, and therefore so
mind-numbingly slow that I quickly left it behind.  Happily, the
Mine's end was the Gnome King's Wine Cellar, so I found tons of booze
to dilute into holy water as well as a cache of gems including some
that actually weren't soft.  I also found a bit of food but no tinning
kit, and managed to gain a pet Deep One which I named Ftahng.

I climbed all the way back up to level 2, made holy water, blessed my
bag of holding and one of my luckstones (I found a second in the
Mine's End), stashed some junk and started to sacrifice.  My pets were
left downstairs so they wouldn't eat much (I was still in a bad spot
with food).  I was surprised to note that I was able to sustain myself
by killing some monsters and sacrificing others now - guess once baby
dragons start showing up in numbers, food becomes less of an issue. 
There's not much that's very filling at low levels.  After 10 minutes
or so of whacking little dogs and assorted baby dragons, Odin gave me
Mojo, whereupon I let out a mighty battlecry of "YEAH BABY, YEAH!" 
Moving to reclaim my pets, I discovered that Ftahng had gone wild, and
Felis had once again become a kitten.  Oh well.  WHACK-CRACKABOOM! 
Goodnight Ftahng.

I therefore began to make final preperations.  I hadn't found a lot of
wands, unfortunately, but the one I did find was a wand of digging. 
Hmm... I was planning on trying to whack the nasties, but dropping
past them might be more realistic for the time being...  I also
visited the armor shop one last time and decided to buy a Hawaiian
Shirt on a whim.  70 GP for a Hawaiian Shirt?!?  Must be enchanted. 
It was.  To +5.  Score one more (admittedly nonvital) piece of the
Ascension kit.  That brought my AC to -19 where it still remains.

Descending into the level from hell, I immediately did the sensible
thing and ran from the stone golem to a spot a few steps from the
stairs, then used the wand of digging to drop through the floor... 
Right into the middle of town.  By this point I was hungry again and
all out of non-tripe non-lizard food.Luckily town had stores. 
Valkyrie needs food badly!  There's a ration there!  ARUM NUMM NUMM! 
What?  You want 180 GP for a food ration?  Lousy rotton shopkeepers...
 If I weren't lawful and probably no match for you, I'd...

Anyway, I also discovered Whisperfeet (artifact speedboots) for sale
in one of the shops in town, but I didn't have nearly enough money for
them, and after the zap I got from picking them up, I decided that the
blisters they'd leave on my feet probably made them not worth the
effort of stealing them.  Plus they'd be just begging to zap me after
an encounter with an incubus or a nymph leaving me with a very
embarassing death.  Death by Whisperfeet.  Ouch.  So I passed on
those, but noted that Speed Boots are apparently Hiking Boots, if I
recall correctly.  That will be useful later on when I... you know...
actually find a pair that won't burn my feet off.  I consoled myself
by purchasing a pair of gauntlets of power and selling my old
unidentified gloves.

I used spoilers for Sokoban because I suck as puzzle games, only to
discover that the third level was the one left unsolved on the
spoilers page.  Lovely.  Rather than trying to figure it out, I spent
an hour looking for a program to do it for me, then painstakingly
transcribed the level into the solver (in a different text format, of
course), and then recorded all of the steps required to victory.  Took
a good 2 hours.  But I solved the puzzle nevertheless and sent the
solution out so nobody else would have to use their brain if they
didn't want to.  Sokoban yielded an amulet of reflection, which is
good because I already had 2 bags of holding by this point.  Sadly, I
lost my last kitty in the zoo after unwisely thwomping a sleeping
yellow light with Mojo without wearinbg a towel.  Turns out I don't
have ESP after all.  Guess my kitties were always faster than me.

Ran into a double bones file on the way where a dwarf had toasted two
promising characters with a wand of fire.  Heh heh heh.  In between
stories about mining, he laughed at their fate.  I took their absurdly
large stores of food and added it to my Sokoban yield in the Bag of
Holding, which was getting awfully full, and swiped the tinning kit
that the archaeologist had.  I stashed all the dangerous stuff
(including a submachinegun) in a nearby chest.

Onward and downward.  I discovered a level full of rats a few more
levels down (10 or 11, I believe it was).  That wouldn't have been any
sweat except for the polymorph trap on that level.  Luckily, the worst
I had to deal with from that was a couple ogres, 2 caterwauls, and a
yellow jelly (which engulfed me and corroded one of my wands.  Oh no,
whatever shall I do?).

The latest thing I've done was bravely assaulting Grund's Stronghold
with only a kitten I tamed previously.  That was quite a battle, let
me tell you.  Before endtering the Stronghold, I had to face a half
dozen or so orcs on the killing fields.  This would have been easy,
except one had a wand of create hordes.  One of these hordebeasts was
a housecat, and it was persuaded to join my side in the battle by a
timely tripe payment.  I was quickly surrounded, with an acid blob and
my two-kitty-army (who spent practically the entire time eating, the
ungrateful buggers) helping to keep the pressure to a managable level.
 After many whomps and bolts of lightning, I stood in a pile of
corpses (which was smaller than you might think because the kitties
ate most of them) having lost only 1/3 of my hit points and screaming
defiance at the drawbridge.

Drawbridge, eh?  I've heard that many people died pathetic deaths to
drawbridges.  Let me give this plenty of space.  A good 8 spaces or so
from the bridge, and my wand of striking fired, smashing the bridge to
pieces.  Excellent!  Now to bridge the moat.  My wand of frost created
a quick and easy ice bridge, which was quickly melted by a soldier
with a wand of fire.  Bastard.  My wand of striking took him out, and
I actually managed to cross the second ice bridge and enter the
fortress.  A long battle ensued, and by the time it was done, Grund
the Orc King had been slain, many trolls had been eaten, and only 5
remained standing.  Myself, two kitties, and the gypsy and nurse that
Grund had been holding captive.  Four treasure chests filled with
cool-looking stuff and over 10,000 GP awaited me when I saved most
recently.

I'm pretty excited here, as this character is doing extremely well. 
I'm beginning to think I might have a chance at ascending, which would
be a first for me.  Of course, there's still quite a way to go, but
things are already beginning to have trouble hurting me, so...  Yeah. 
I think this character might make it.  My present plan is to continue
downward until I reach level 14, then go back up and clear out level 5
and the Mine King stage.  I should be able to actually play the
protection game now that I've got 15,000 GP or so, plus probably that
much again in stashed gems.  Finding another altar or temple sooner
rather than later would be nice, however.

We'll see.  Thanks again for the advice, everyone.

Eric "Critic of the Dawn"

